Ansible Ad-Hoc Commands

Today I learned about Ad-Hoc commands, which are quick, one-line commands used to perform tasks instantly — without writing a playbook.
Think of them as “quick automation shortcuts.”

🔹 ansible Command

The ansible command is used to:
Run tasks directly from terminal
Target specific hosts or groups
Execute modules instantly

It’s useful for:
Quick checks
Emergency fixes
Testing connectivity
Ad-hoc commands are great for small tasks.

🔹 ping Module
The ping module:
Tests connectivity
Confirms SSH access
Verifies Ansible communication
It does not ping like ICMP, instead it checks if Ansible can execute Python on the node.
Very useful for troubleshooting.

🔹 shell vs command
Both run commands on remote nodes, but:

command :- 
Safer
Does not use shell
No pipes or redirection

shell :-
Uses shell interpreter
Supports pipes & variables
More flexible
Use command when possible for better security.

🔹 file & service Modules
These modules help in configuration tasks:

file module :-
Create/delete files
Change permissions
Manage directories

service module :-
Start/stop services
Restart applications
Enable services at boot
These are commonly used in real production automation.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Ad-hoc = quick automation.
 Modules perform actions.
 Use wisely, automate smartly.
